  "notes": "Honky-tonk and western swing played on the \"guit-steel\", the purpose-built hybrid of a six-string neck over a full lap-steel neck, and the instrument is the entry's defining parameter. The research asks for a lead voice that switches abruptly within one solo between \"sharp-attack Telecaster-style double-stops and chicken-picked staccato\" and \"legato, portamento-heavy lap-steel glissandi\", so the melody is authored staccato with a third-heavy interval spread (0.38, for the double-stops), a wide 55–84 register for the instrument's stated \"enormous frequency range\", and an octave-jump probability of 0.05–0.14 standing in for the neck switch. Drums follow the documented constraint that the kit is \"deliberately minimal because the guit-steel occupies an enormous frequency range — model low cymbal density and a narrow, dry snare\": the inherited ride lane falls to 0–2 hits at −12 dB, the open hat to 0.08, the hat fill to 0.28, the velocity tiers to 100–120 over 70–88, and fills move out to 8/16 bars. The kick is the entry's shuffled two-beat at the 101–121 band. Harmony is the entry's own list — I–IV–V with V7, twelve-bar frames, I–I7–IV turns, and the western-swing ii–V and VI7–II7–V7–I turnarounds — written as degrees with the sevenths in extensions. Keys are constrained by the documented fact that the steel neck is tuned to an open chord, so E/A/D/G carry the weight. The vocal is the documented \"deep, dry, deadpan baritone with almost no ornament — the vocal is a straight man to the instrument\", which is why the counter (the steel neck answering) carries more density than the melody's own ornament. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are swing.amount 0.66, all velocity tiers, ghost probability and positions, and the ride/hat densities that express \"low cymbal density\"; the section's swing-ratio percentages are [LOW-CONF] by their author's own mark and the BPM readings are bpmdatabase values, [LOW-CONF] as a class. Confidence is medium because the research rates the entry medium-high and states its harmony is generalised from the western-swing frame [LOW-CONF].",
